@lang en
IMPORT prelude

-- Nouns for row IL-35: the consumer price index readings (published data) and what the Income Tax Ordinance
-- s 1 / s 120B and the National Insurance Law s 334(a)(1) take from them. DECLARE only; no rules.

§ `Nouns of the consumer price index`

-- A calendar month, by its year and its number (1 for January to 12 for December).
DECLARE `A calendar month` HAS
    `year of the month`              IS A NUMBER
    `number of the month in the year` IS A NUMBER

-- One reading of the index as the Central Bureau of Statistics published it, in the base in force that month.
-- PUBLISHED DATA, NOT LAW (cpi-il35-published-figures.l4).
DECLARE `A published reading` HAS
    `the year the reading is of`                                IS A NUMBER
    `the month the reading is of`                               IS A NUMBER
    `the base year of the reading`                              IS A NUMBER
    `the value of the reading`                                  IS A NUMBER
    `the percent the Bureau published for the month`            IS A NUMBER
    `the percent the Bureau published for the twelve months`    IS A NUMBER

-- A period of whole months: it begins at the start of its first month and ends at the end of its last.
-- The Ordinance's "tax year" (s 1, a period of twelve consecutive months beginning on 1 January) is one.
DECLARE `A period of whole months` HAS
    `the first month of the period` IS A `A calendar month`
    `the last month of the period`  IS A `A calendar month`

-- s 1 defines the rate of rise of the index by two readings, divided by the earlier one. A reading in one base
-- and a reading in another are not on one scale until one is restated; the text does not say how the two are compared
-- (fork F1, a switch, ruled by Meng 2026-10-08 SHRUG).
DECLARE `A reading of how readings of two bases are compared` IS ONE OF
    `readings of two bases are compared by linking them through the Bureau's coefficients`
    `a rise over twelve months is the percent the Bureau published for the twelve months`
    `a rise across a change of base is declined`

-- s 120B(e)(2): on 1 January 2028 the 1 January 2024 amounts are adjusted, "the index in the previous tax year" being
-- "the index of tax year 2027". Which rise that is, is fork F2 (a switch).
DECLARE `A reading of the rise s 120B(e)(2) takes on 1 January 2028` IS ONE OF
    `the rise of the index in tax year 2027 alone`
    `the rise of the index from the start of tax year 2024 to the end of tax year 2027`
    `the period of the rise on 1 January 2028 is declined`

-- The pair of readings a rate of rise is taken from, restated on one scale so that s 1's arithmetic
-- (the difference over the earlier reading) applies to them directly. The field names are the ones row IL-03 uses
-- for its input `Index readings for a period`, so a caller can hand the two values across.
DECLARE `Index readings on one scale for a period` HAS
    `index last published before the start of the period` IS A NUMBER
    `index last published before the end of the period`   IS A NUMBER
